repository-code: >-
  https://github.com/archaeothommy/ReproducibilityArchaeometallurgy
abstract: >-
  To test the current state of reproducibility in the
  archaeological sciences, a bibliographic survey was
  performed on articles publishing compositional and
  isotopic data in the field’s major journals between
  November 2025 and January 2026. While restricted to
  archaeometallurgy, it can be assumed that the findings are
  applicable to other subfields because of the featured
  analytical methods and the similar considerations in
  sampling and data processing. A reproducibility index was
  developed to provide a comparable and quantitative
  assessment. The index follows a practitioner’s
  perspective, combining elements of analytical and
  computational reproducibility. While about 50% of the
  articles are fully reproducible in each stage, pronounced
  differences can be observed between stages. Overall data
  quality would from benefit from increased reproducibility
  of analytical protocols and visualisations, improved
  handling of analytical uncertainty, transparency in data
  treatment, and the adoption of standardised terminologies,
  as well as more attention to the accuracy of data
  availability statements. Recommendations are provided for
  each topic. The adoption of data publications and data
  processing pipelines are identified as the most impactful
  measures because they address several of the identified
  target areas at once.
keywords:
  - Archaeometallurgy
  - Data quality
  - Reproducibility
  - Literature survey
  - Publishing
